1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,287
Bear's Notes
One Electron Interferes With Itself

2
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:08,133
Fire electrons at two slits one at a time — each arrives as a single dot.

3
00:00:08,410 --> 00:00:12,763
Ten in, the dots look random; common sense says two heaps, one behind each slit.

4
00:00:13,010 --> 00:00:18,263
Seventy thousand in: stripes — bright and dark bands no two-heap picture allows.

5
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:23,000
Stripes are what waves do: ripples through both slits overlap and cancel.

6
00:00:23,230 --> 00:00:26,950
Particles can't do that — a ball takes one slit and joins its heap.

7
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:31,757
And these electrons flew alone: never a second one in the machine to interfere with.

8
00:00:31,950 --> 00:00:35,970
So each electron's wave goes through both slits and interferes with itself.

9
00:00:36,210 --> 00:00:40,997
The dot is where it's found; the stripes map where finding it is likely — psi squared.

10
00:00:41,070 --> 00:00:45,057
Peek at which slit it took, and the stripes vanish — information kills them.

11
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:50,853
Molecules of two thousand atoms paint the same stripes; your wavelength is just too small to show it.

12
00:00:50,950 --> 00:01:01,003
The full story — the buildup, de Broglie's wavelength, the forbidden bands — is in the long video. Thanks for watching — more at youtube dot com, slash, at Nik Bear Brown.
